problem:
Problem description
- There is a single batch packing and transportation planning problem over a one-period planning horizon $$\mathcal{T}$$, with goods indexed by $$i \in \mathcal{I}$$ and candidate containers indexed by $$k \in \mathcal{K}$$.
- The product set is generalized as $$\mathcal{I}$$, with one available quantity parameter $$q_i$$ and one unit-weight parameter $$w_i$$ for each product $$i \in \mathcal{I}$$.
- The container fleet is modeled by a sufficiently large candidate set $$\mathcal{K}$$. Each used container has a maximum payload capacity $$U$$ and must also satisfy a minimum utilized load requirement $$L$$.
- Some products may have intra-container packaging rules. These rules are represented by a set of compatibility requirements $$\mathcal{R}$$. In this problem class, one product may trigger a minimum accompanying quantity of another product within the same container, while the supporting product may still appear without the trigger product.
- Some products may also have per-used-container minimum load requirements. In this problem class, a designated product may be required to appear in at least a fixed minimum quantity in every container that is used.
- The planning goal is to transport the entire required batch using the fewest possible number of containers while respecting container capacity, minimum-load, inventory, and packaging compatibility requirements.
- The available goods for the concrete instance are the five products $$A$$, $$B$$, $$C$$, $$D$$, and $$E$$.
- The business rules for the concrete instance are:
  - Each used container may carry at most $$60$$ tons in total.
  - Each used container must carry at least $$18$$ tons in total.
  - The available quantities are $$120$$ units of $$A$$, $$90$$ units of $$B$$, $$300$$ units of $$C$$, $$90$$ units of $$D$$, and $$120$$ units of $$E$$.
  - The unit weights are $$0.5$$ tons for $$A$$, $$1$$ ton for $$B$$, $$0.4$$ tons for $$C$$, $$0.6$$ tons for $$D$$, and $$0.65$$ tons for $$E$$.
  - If a container contains any units of $$A$$, then that same container must also contain at least $$1$$ unit of $$C$$.
  - Product $$C$$ may be loaded without product $$A$$.
  - Every used container must contain at least $$12$$ units of $$D$$.
  - All required goods must be assigned to containers without exceeding the available inventory.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$y_k$$, a binary container-usage indicator for each $$k \in \mathcal{K}$$, where $$y_k = 1$$ means container $$k$$ is used.
  - $$x_{ik}$$, the integer number of units of product $$i \in \mathcal{I}$$ loaded into container $$k \in \mathcal{K}$$.
  - The total number of used containers, which is the objective value to be minimized.
  - The packing allocation by container, including the quantity of each product placed in each container.

